# Audits not yet in the mutation corpus (GUARDRAILS_SPEC.md section 17, item 6)
#
# A background mutation sweep bypassed all 42 audits. The shared layer
# (crates/source-audit/src/{resolve,ast}.rs) closes the structural causes for
# every audit at once; a fixture here is what *shows* a given audit rejects
# its sweep mutation rather than merely being ported onto the hardened layer.
#
# This list can only shrink: `every_audit_has_rejection_fixtures_or_is_on_the_shrinking_list`
# fails if an audit here gains three fixtures without being removed, and fails
# if an audit not here has fewer than three.
#
# 2026-09-22: emptied. Every registered audit has at least three rejection
# fixtures, including an alias/rename variant and (where the audit is about a
# result rather than a call) a discarded-result variant. Writing them found
# three real holes in the shared layer, not just in the audits:
#
#   * `ast::functions` attributed macro literals by function *name*, so a
#     second, wrong `files_schema` inherited the real one's
#     `vec![Field::new("mod_time_min", ..)]` and passed;
#   * `ast::function` returned the first definition, so "the function named X
#     must have property P" was satisfied by whichever X came first;
#   * `Func` carried no signature, so a coupling written as
#     `fn f(d: &dyn locations::Detector)` was invisible to every body rule.
#
# Keep it empty. A new audit lands with its fixtures, or it lands here with a
# reason and a date.
